Compact EEG decoders summarise each window as log power per channel, the
diagonal of its spatial covariance. How channels covary is discarded, and that
structure carries movement-intent information the diagonal does not.

We add a tangent-space branch beside the log-power stem. Each window's
covariance is shrunk, mapped to the tangent space at a reference the network
maintains from the input alone, then projected into the classifier. No labels
are used at inference and the reference keeps updating on the test stream.
Across five cohorts and 51 participants, three seeds each, the branch raises
balanced accuracy on four: $+0.064$ on a 20-participant motor-execution cohort
held out from all development ($p=8.8\times10^{-5}$, higher in 20 of
20), $+0.045$ on a lower-limb exoskeleton cohort ($p=0.22$), $+0.040$ on BCI
Competition IV-2a ($p=0.020$) and $+0.021$ on a second exoskeleton cohort
($p=0.047$). On those four it has the highest mean balanced accuracy among eight published decoders trained in the same pipeline, untuned.

On the fifth it costs $0.175$, and that failure is predictable before
training. The reference is a running mean over incoming windows, safe only while its effective memory $\mu=N/m$ outlasts a class block. Blocks last 214 windows there against a memory of 160, and 1 to 18 where the branch helps. Both are fixed before training, and separate all five cohorts. Moving the memory across the block length switches the effect
off and on: $+0.045$ at 160 windows, $+0.048$ at 40 and $-0.076$ at 8. A stronger prescription setting the rate from the block length was pre-specified before the run and falsified.
